\begin_layout Title
Game Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 8, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Game theory is a methodology used to study strategic decision making and
 the interactions between agents in strategic situations.
 The payoffs of agents in noncooperative game theory depend not only on
 their own actions, but also on the actions of other agents, which means
 the beliefs of players are endogendous.
 The techniques of game theory can be applied in a wide variety of contexts,
 and they have become pervasive in economic research.
 However, these devices often conflict in determining reasonable equilibria
 for economic games, and they offer differerent positive explanations concerning
 observed behavior.
 Thus, game theory offers a wide variety of opportunities for experimental
 research, especially for the purpose of evaluating predictions against
 alternative theories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Van Huyck, Battalio, and Beil (1990) is a prime example of running experiments
 in order to analyze the different predictions offered by competing equilibrium
 models.
 They tend to focus on the tools offered by deductive equilibrium methods,
 such as Rational Expectations or Bayesian Nash Equilibrium.
 The researchers study a class of tacit pure coordination games with multiple
 equilibria.
 The experiments provide evidence on how human subjects make decisions under
 conditions of strategic uncertainty.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another area of study that has been a chief concern to both game theorists
 and experimentalists is markets with asymmetric information, especially
 those that give rise to the problem of moral hazard.
 A situation in which moral hazard occurs is when both sellers' costs and
 buyers' values depend on product quality.
 The sellers know the quality of their product, while the quality cannot
 be directly observed by buyers.
 Forsythe, Isaac, and Palfrey (1989) present results from experiments that
 examine this situation.
 In their design, sellers had the opportunity to voluntarily reveal information
 concerning the quality of the item, or they could decide to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

blind bid
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the item.
 A good predictor of behavior in these markets is a sequential equilibrium
 model where buyers 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

assume the worst.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 While allocations were ex post efficient, it took some time for the equilibrium
 to be achieved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For dynamic games, backwards induction is the primary method used to determine
 reasonable Nash equilibria, also known as subgame perfect Nash equilibria.
 However, the centipede game is a significant example of the possible paradoxes
 that could arise when applying backwards induction.
 Palacious-Huerta and Volij (2009) conduct both field and labatory experiments
 of the centipede game with chess players and students in order to investigate
 the assumption of common knowledge of rationality, which, in turn, implies
 the backward induction outcome.
 In both the field and in the lab, outcomes are close to the subgame-perfect
 equilibrium prediciton when chess players play against one another.
 Also, when students play against chess players, outcomes are much closer
 to the subgame-perfect equilibrium than when students play against other
 students.
 These findings solidify the theory that the predictive power of subgame-perfect
 equilibrium hinges mainly on knowledge of players' rationalisty.
\end_layout

